      "why_read": "Understand how query transformation pipelines process inputs to improve retrieval performance and accuracy.",
      "authors": [
        "gvsg-rs"
      ],
      "image": "/infographics/08-hn-49984834.jpg",
      "content": "Caching relational queries typically requires manual cache invalidation or severe application-level compromises. Incremental view maintenance offers a cleaner path, but transforming arbitrary SQL into maintainable dataflow graphs is notoriously difficult.\n\nReadySet relies on an internal query transformation pipeline that takes raw incoming SQL, parses it into an AST, and normalizes complex subqueries, joins, and aggregations. The engine transforms these normalized trees into streaming relational operators capable of updating state incrementally as upstream writes arrive.\n\nHandling complex edge cases such as outer joins, non-deterministic functions, and nested expressions requires multiple optimization passes. Each pass systematically rewrites query nodes into equivalent, cacheable primitives while verifying that relational semantics remain identical to the primary database.\n\nUnderstanding query transformation pipelines is essential for anyone designing high-throughput caching proxies or streaming query engines."
    },
